# Line-ending policy: every tracked text file is LF, in the index and on disk.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows and deployed on Linux, and `core.autocrlf=true`
# rewrites text files to CRLF at checkout. Python and the YAML/TOML/SQL parsers
# all tolerate that, so it stays invisible until something on the Linux side
# reads a byte it cannot ignore. Two cases have already bitten:
#
#   * `docker/entrypoint.sh` is executed by name, so the kernel reads its shebang
#     literally: CRLF turns `#!/bin/sh` into `#!/bin/sh\r`, exec then looks for an
#     interpreter called `/bin/sh<CR>`, and the container dies with "no such file
#     or directory" -- naming a missing interpreter, not a missing file, which is
#     why it reads as a phantom.
#   * `config/profiles/*.env` are sourced by `scripts/validate-*.sh`; a CRLF
#     checkout gives every value a trailing carriage return.
#
# `eol=lf` pins the bytes, so the checkout that builds the image and the one CI
# builds from cannot disagree. `text=auto` lets git keep real binaries (the
# diagrams' PNGs, any `.so`) untouched -- they contain NUL, so they are never
# detected as text.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Spelled out, because these are the files executed by name and the shebang is
# what breaks. `tests/test_deploy_manifest_*`-style checks pin the deployment
# files; the `line-endings` CI job re-materialises a Windows checkout and fails
# if any tracked file comes back CRLF.
*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
